-- title:
--   §2, §2.2, §6.1: min f = 0, argmin f, the least-norm subgradient ∂⁰f, subgradient curves χ̇ ∈ −∂f(χ), and the length of a curve
-- statement:
--   Let $H$ be a real Hilbert space and $f : H \to (-\infty, +\infty]$. The convex subdifferential of $f$ at $x$ is $\partial f(x) = \{u \in H : f(y) \ge f(x) + \langle u, y - x\rangle \text{ for all } y \in H\}$; it is empty when $f(x) = +\infty$. This module fixes the objects of §2 and §6.1 of Bolte–Nguyen–Peypouquet–Suter used in the characterization of Łojasiewicz inequalities.
--
--   1. **Normalization.** $f$ satisfies $\min f = 0$ when $f(y) \ge 0$ for every $y \in H$ and $f(x) = 0$ for some $x$.
--
--   2. **Minimizers.** $S = \operatorname{argmin} f = \{x \in H : f(x) \le f(y) \text{ for all } y \in H\}$. Under $\min f = 0$ this is $\{x : f(x) = 0\}$.
--
--   3. **Least-norm subgradient.** A vector $p$ is the least-norm subgradient $\partial^0 f(x)$ of $f$ at $x$ when $p \in \partial f(x)$ and $\|p\| \le \|q\|$ for every $q \in \partial f(x)$. Such a $p$ exists exactly when $x \in \operatorname{dom} \partial f$.
--
--   4. **Subgradient curves.** Given $x \in H$, a curve $\chi : [0, \infty) \to H$ is a subgradient curve of $f$ issued from $x$ when
--      - $\chi(0) = x$;
--      - $\chi$ is continuous on $[0, \infty)$ and absolutely continuous on every compact interval $[a, b]$ with $0 < a \le b$, and, when $f(x) < +\infty$, on every interval $[0, b]$ with $b \ge 0$;
--      - for almost every $t > 0$, $\chi$ is differentiable at $t$ and
--      $$\dot\chi(t) \in -\partial f(\chi(t)).$$
--
--   5. **Length.** For $t < s$, the length of $\chi$ between $t$ and $s$ is
--   $$\operatorname{length}(\chi, t, s) = \int_t^s \|\dot\chi(\tau)\|\, d\tau \in [0, +\infty].$$
--
--   Subgradient curves are the trajectories of the nonlinear contraction semigroup generated by $-\partial f$; their length is the quantity that the KL inequality controls.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $f$ takes values in `EReal`, and the subdifferential is the published `subgrad` of `MoreauProx.Characterization.GammaZero`. The least-norm subgradient is a predicate, not a choice function. A curve is a map `ℝ → H` whose values at negative times are ignored. Absolute continuity is Mathlib's `AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval`, required on intervals $[0, b]$ only when $x \in \operatorname{dom} f$ and otherwise on intervals $[a, b] \subset (0, \infty)$: for a starting point $x \in \overline{\operatorname{dom} f} \setminus \operatorname{dom} f$ the Brézis solution need not have finite length near $t = 0$. The length is a lower Lebesgue integral with values in $[0, +\infty]$ of `‖deriv χ τ‖`; `deriv χ` differs from $\dot\chi$ only on a null set.
-- source:
--   arXiv:1510.08234v3 (Bolte, Nguyen, Peypouquet, Suter, From error bounds to the complexity of first-order descent methods for convex functions), §2, p. 4 (S = argmin f, min f = 0, ∂f); §2.1, pp. 4–5 (∂⁰f); §2.2, p. 5 (the differential inclusion); §6.1, p. 25 (length(χx, t, s))

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MoreauProx_Characterization_GammaZero
open scoped InnerProductSpace
open MoreauProx.Characterization

namespace ErrBoundCplx.KLEB

/-- §2, p. 4: the standing normalization `min f = 0`: `f` is nonnegative and attains `0`
(so `argmin f` is nonempty). -/
def MinIsZero {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H]
    (f : H → EReal) : Prop :=
  (∀ y, 0 ≤ f y) ∧ ∃ x, f x = 0

/-- §2, p. 4: `S = argmin f`, the set of global minimizers of `f`. -/
def argminSet {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H]
    (f : H → EReal) : Set H :=
  {x | ∀ y, f x ≤ f y}

/-- §2.1, pp. 4–5: `p = ∂⁰f(x)`, the least-norm element of the subdifferential `∂f(x)`
(stated as a predicate; such a `p` exists exactly when `x ∈ dom ∂f`). -/
def IsLeastNormSubgrad {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H]
    (f : H → EReal) (x p : H) : Prop :=
  p ∈ subgrad f x ∧ ∀ q ∈ subgrad f x, ‖p‖ ≤ ‖q‖

/-- §2.2, p. 5: `χ` is a solution of the subgradient differential inclusion
`χ̇(t) ∈ −∂f(χ(t))` for almost every `t > 0`, `χ(0) = x`. The curve is continuous on
`[0, ∞)` and absolutely continuous on every compact interval `[a, b] ⊂ (0, ∞)` (Brézis'
notion of a strong solution issued from `x ∈ cl(dom f)`); when `x ∈ dom f` it is moreover
absolutely continuous on every `[0, b]`, i.e. on `[0, ∞)` as the page states. Its derivative
exists and satisfies the inclusion at almost every `t > 0`. Values of `χ` at negative times play
no role. -/
def IsSubgradCurve {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H]
    (f : H → EReal) (x : H) (χ : ℝ → H) : Prop :=
  χ 0 = x ∧ ContinuousOn χ (Set.Ici 0) ∧
    (∀ a b : ℝ, 0 < a → a ≤ b → AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval χ a b) ∧
    (f x ≠ ⊤ → ∀ b : ℝ, 0 ≤ b → AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval χ 0 b) ∧
    ∀ᵐ t : ℝ, 0 < t → ∃ v : H, HasDerivAt χ v t ∧ -v ∈ subgrad f (χ t)

/-- §6.1, p. 25: `length(χ, t, s) = ∫ₜˢ ‖χ̇(τ)‖ dτ`, computed as a lower Lebesgue integral in
`[0, ∞]` (so it is never a junk value; `deriv χ` differs from `χ̇` only on a null set). -/
noncomputable def curveLength {H : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup H] [InnerProductSpace ℝ H]
    (χ : ℝ → H) (t s : ℝ) : ENNReal :=
  ∫⁻ τ in Set.Ioc t s, ‖deriv χ τ‖ₑ

end ErrBoundCplx.KLEB


